            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Composes two rotations into one: the product a * b turns by b first, then by a. Both are quaternions, scalar first: w x y z. Either can be inverted before multiplying, so the same node takes a rotation away as well as adding one.\n\nThis is how an offset is applied: a calibration correction times a sensor's reading gives the corrected reading. Many at once work alike: a row of one offset per sensor times a whole take of frames corrects every frame of every sensor. With tensors in, the result stays on their device, and a t.param offset keeps its trail for t.optimize.\n\nThe product is not flipped to keep its w positive, so a smoothly turning stream stays smooth.\n\nSYNTAX:\nquaternion_multiply\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\na:\nThe rotation applied second. Sends the product.\n\nb:\nThe rotation applied first, held. Lists, arrays and tensors broadcast against a: one quaternion against many, or (sensors, 4) against (frames, sensors, 4).\n\ninvert a, invert b:\nOptions, under the node. Use the opposite rotation of that side: invert a with b gives the turn from a to b.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\na * b:\nThe combined rotation, as a tensor.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n0.7071 0 0 0.7071 is a quarter turn about z. A quarter turn after a quarter turn is a half turn: 0 0 0 1.\n\nRELATED:\nquaternion_relative gives the turn from one rotation to another. quaternion_norm keeps a quaternion unit length. swing_twist splits a rotation into pointing and twist.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Composes two rotations into one: the product a * b turns by b first, then by a. Both are quaternions, scalar first: w x y z. Either can be inverted before multiplying, so the same node takes a rotation away as well as adding one.\n\nThis is how an offset is applied: a calibration correction times a sensor's reading gives the corrected reading. Many at once work alike: a row of one offset per sensor times a whole take of frames corrects every frame of every sensor. With tensors in, the result stays on their device, and a t.param offset keeps its trail for t.optimize.\n\nThe product is not flipped to keep its w positive, so a smoothly turning stream stays smooth.\n\nSYNTAX:\nquaternion_multiply\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\na:\nThe rotation applied second. Sends the product.\n\nb:\nThe rotation applied first, held. Lists, arrays and tensors broadcast against a: one quaternion against many, or (sensors, 4) against (frames, sensors, 4).\n\ninvert a, invert b:\nOptions, under the node. Use the opposite rotation of that side: invert a with b gives the turn from a to b.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\na * b:\nThe combined rotation, as a tensor.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n0.7071 0 0 0.7071 is a quarter turn about z. A quarter turn after a quarter turn is a half turn: 0 0 0 1.\n\nRELATED:\nquaternion_relative gives the turn from one rotation to another. quaternion_norm keeps a quaternion unit length. swing_twist splits a rotation into pointing and twist."
        },
